{
 "_doc": "How a line is said (#1316): each tag's face, beat, gestures and what the men listening do; the gestures' dials; how a script's free-text `delivery` note reads as a tag. Read by Delivery (src/people/delivery.gd) and played by LineGesture (src/bodies/line_gesture.gd). Degrees are HumanBody's pose axes: chest/abdomen x + leans back, - forward; head x + up, y + to his left, z + tips to his right; upper_arm x + raises it forward, z + out (the right; the left mirrored); forearm x + bends the elbow; clavicle x + lifts the shoulder. A gesture's `keys` are [seconds from its beat, {segment: degrees}] eased between, nothing before the first or after the last; `span: line` times them as shares of the line's voice instead (0 its first sound, 1 its last), seconds past it after 1. `osc`: a shake on top, {hz, segs, from, to} (seconds about the beat). `hand`: r, l or both: the arm's segments only where that hand is free (a busy right hand gives the gesture to the left, mirrored); `reach`: knee (the hand's IK to his knee, `lift` m above it before the beat). `walk`: may be done walking (head and face only). `look`: away, down, listener (his eyes to the man he's talking to), each_other (a listener's to another listener), speaker. Face shapes: brows_up, brows_down, squint, scowl, smile, smile_l, smile_r are on every talking face today; the others (ARKit names: jawForward, mouthPress*, eyeWide*, browInnerUp, mouthFrown*, noseSneer*) are the hook for the MetaHuman's (#692) and do nothing on a face without them.",
